        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(qa): avoid touching operator state during private QA runs",
        "body": "Closes #111432\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where developers running private QA commands would have the QA launcher read, validate, and migrate their configured OpenClaw state before the disposable QA gateway established its own isolation.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe QA command subtree now declares the complete startup boundary it needs: no operator config guard, no operator plugin registry load, and no operator-managed proxy bootstrap. This keeps source-checkout QA orchestration separate from real operator state while leaving the disposable gateway and its explicit network/model configuration unchanged.\n\n## User Impact\n\nDevelopers can run private QA suites, coverage queries, and live QA flows without the launcher opening or modifying their normal OpenClaw state database. There is no end-user behavior change.\n\nAI-assisted: yes. I reviewed the startup policy, proxy bootstrap, config observation, and QA launcher paths and understand the resulting ownership boundary.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Focused CLI tests: 82 tests passed across `command-path-policy`, `preaction`, and `run-main` after rebasing onto current `main`.\n- Autoreview: clean, no accepted/actionable findings.\n- Blacksmith changed-file gate: formatting, core/core-test typechecks, lint, dependency and architecture guards all passed.\n- Packaged private-QA build: `OPENCLAW_BUILD_PRIVATE_QA=1 pnpm build` passed.\n- Linux black-box proof: ran `openclaw qa coverage` with intentionally malformed operator config and a sentinel state tree; the command returned the expected scenario match, emitted no config/Doctor/state-database diagnostics, and left the operator-state tree and file hashes unchanged.\n- Remote proof: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29690529203\n",
        "branch": "codex/qa-host-state-isolation",
